1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:33,500
HomeRelay: ask for help, keep responsibility clear.
A request does not transfer ownership until the recipient accepts.

2
00:00:33,500 --> 00:01:02,600
Alex creates a household errand with an owner and a due time.
The browser asks for confirmation before saving it.

3
00:01:02,600 --> 00:01:22,500
The task is saved. FastAPI validates changes; SQLite persists them.
Task versions reject stale changes; request IDs prevent duplicate creation.

4
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:40,100
Alex proposes a handoff to Mira and confirms it.
Alex remains responsible while the request waits for a response.

5
00:01:40,100 --> 00:01:52,600
Mira sees the pending request and can accept or decline.
Nicknames select a demo actor; they do not authenticate individual people.

6
00:01:52,600 --> 00:02:08,400
Mira confirms acceptance. The stored owner changes to Mira.
This is the actual running application with fictional household data.

7
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:23,600
The page is reloaded; Mira is still the owner.
Six real HTTP MCP tools expose the same validated store operations.

8
00:02:23,600 --> 00:02:28,400
Mira confirms that the errand is complete.
Nine automated tests also cover REST, MCP, retries and concurrent edits.

9
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:41,510
Completed tasks and an ordered history preserve the workflow.
AI-assisted local prototype; no Alexa connection or field study is claimed.
Next: individual authentication, household isolation and real user evaluation.
